{"card":{"card_id":"ma-c-4ef2f136a73ae01fcb4b","statement":"\\begin{NB} Why the coefficient is in $\\CC[\\ft]^{W_{\\la+\\mu}}$? Sasha wrote that it is easy to see.... \\end{NB} Let us check that $a_{\\la,\\mu} fg\\in \\CC[\\ft]^{W_{\\la+\\mu}}$. From the description of $a_{\\la,\\mu}$ (see <<ref:relation>>), it is clear that $a_{\\la,\\mu}\\in \\CC[\\ft]^W$. Note also that $W_\\la$ is the subgroup of $W$ generated by simple reflections $s_i$ such that the corresponding simple roots $\\alpha_i$ are perpendicular to $\\la$. \\begin{NB} Suppose $w\\in W_\\la$. If $\\alpha\\in\\Delta^+\\cap w(\\Delta^-)$, we have $0\\le \\langle \\la, \\alpha\\rangle = \\langle w\\la,\n        \\alpha\\rangle = \\langle \\la, w\\alpha\\rangle \\le 0$. Therefore $\\langle \\la,\\alpha\\rangle = 0$. Now consider a reduced expression of $w$. \\end{NB} Therefore we have $W_{\\la+\\mu} = W_{\\la} \\cap W_\\mu$, and hence $fg\\in \\CC[\\ft]^{W_{\\la+\\mu}}$.","source_id":"arXiv:1601.03586","cluster":"representation_theory","statement_sha256":"79f095eea8416b36fe642faf2ad713a4f956c3c35a7b8f215eca894e5aed5364","kind":"remark","label":"Remark 55","ref_key":"","name":"","order":82,"deps":[{"ref":"relation","card_id":"ma-c-4d3e469eb87ef211a840","label":"Equation relation","resolved":true,"target":"card"}],"cite_keys":[],"provenance":{"source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.03586","harvest_mode":"arxiv_eprint","payload_sha256":"b2e79974e6e250f264382e70c5d3296d4abfc8d5c86da0ff1e8d9db5aafed33f","line":7679,"origin":"latex_env","retrieved_at":"2026-09-26T15:38:02+08:00"},"wash":{"version":"cardwash.v3","stages":["segment","purify","classify","dedupe","emit"],"numbering":"document_order_with_label_heuristic","detail":{"stripped_proof":false,"chars":824,"math_hints":110}}},"paper":{"source_id":"arXiv:1601.03586","title":"Towards a mathematical definition of Coulomb branches of $3$-dimensional $\\mathcal N=4$ gauge theories, II","abstract":"Consider the $3$-dimensional $\\mathcal N=4$ supersymmetric gauge theory associated with a compact Lie group $G_c$ and its quaternionic representation $\\mathbf M$. Physicists study its Coulomb branch, which is a noncompact hyper-Kähler manifold with an $\\mathrm{SU}(2)$-action, possibly with singularities. We give a mathematical definition of the Coulomb branch as an affine algebraic variety with $\\mathbb C^\\times$-action when $\\mathbf M$ is of a form $\\mathbf N\\oplus\\mathbf N^*$, as the second step of the proposal given in arXiv:1503.03676.","authors":["Alexander Braverman","Michael Finkelberg","Hiraku Nakajima"],"year":"2016","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.03586","cluster":"representation_theory","macros":{"RR":"{\\mathbb R}","bN":"\\mathbf N","SL":"\\operatorname{\\rm SL}","cO":"\\mathcal O","cal":"\\mathcal","Bbb":"\\mathbb","frak":"\\mathfrak","labelenumi":"\\textup{(\\theenumi)}","mathcal":"\\cal","mathbb":"\\Bbb","mathfrak":"\\frak","theenumi":"\\alph{enumi}","thmref":"\\ref{#1}","secref":"\\ref{#1}","lemref":"\\ref{#1}","propref":"\\ref{#1}","corref":"\\ref{#1}","subsecref":"\\ref{#1}","remref":"\\ref{#1}","defeq":"\\overset{\\operatorname{\\scriptstyle def.}}{=}","CC":"{\\mathbb C}","ZZ":"{\\mathbb Z}","QQ":"{\\mathbb Q}","proj":"{\\mathbb P}","CP":"\\proj","SU":"\\operatorname{\\rm SU}","GL":"\\operatorname{GL}","PGL":"\\operatorname{PGL}","U":"\\operatorname{\\rm U}","grpSp":"\\operatorname{\\rm Sp}","algsl":"\\operatorname{\\mathfrak{sl}}","su":"\\operatorname{\\mathfrak{su}}","gl":"\\operatorname{\\mathfrak{gl}}","g":"{\\mathfrak g}","Spec":"\\operatorname{Spec}\\nolimits","Proj":"\\operatorname{Proj}\\nolimits","End":"\\operatorname{End}","Hom":"\\operatorname{Hom}","Ker":"\\operatorname{Ker}","Coker":"\\operatorname{Coker}","Ima":"\\operatorname{Im}","dim":"\\mathop{\\text{\\rm dim}}\\nolimits","codim":"\\mathop{\\text{\\rm codim}}\\nolimits","rank":"\\operatorname{rank}","tr":"\\operatorname{tr}","id":"\\operatorname{id}","HH":"\\mathbb H","dslash":"/\\!\\!/","bM":"\\mathbf M","shfO":"\\mathcal O","la":